DEEP = {
"foguang":{
"t":"Fly into Taiyuan Wusu Airport (TYN, IATA code). From Taiyuan: hire a private car with driver -- this is essential. Current rate is 600-800 CNY per day including fuel. The drive takes 4 hours each way on the G55 expressway then S310 provincial road -- the last 40km is a narrow mountain road with hairpin turns, fully paved but steep. There is NO bus service to Foguang Temple. The nearest town Doucun (豆村) is 10km away and has no taxi service. Your driver will wait while you visit. If coming from Beijing: fly or HSR to Taiyuan (2.5h HSR, 200 CNY), then proceed as above. From Xi'an: HSR to Taiyuan (3.5h, 180 CNY).",
"s":"Doucun village (10km from temple): basic farmhouse guesthouses called nongjiale (农家乐), 80-150 CNY/night, shared bathrooms, no English. Most travelers day-trip from Taiyuan and return same day. Taiyuan hotels: Pullman Taiyuan near Wuyi Square (500-600 CNY/night, international standard, English-speaking staff, best mid-range option); Taiyuan Wanda Realm (400-500 CNY/night, connected to shopping mall with restaurants); Hanting Hotel near railway station (160-200 CNY/night, clean budget chain, Chinese only). Book via Trip.com or Ctrip for foreigner-friendly hotels.",
"f":"Shanxi cuisine is wheat-based and hearty. Must-try dishes: dao xiao mian (刀削面, knife-cut noodles in lamb broth -- the signature Shanxi dish, available at any noodle shop for 15-25 CNY); guo you rou (过油肉, oil-braised pork strips with wood ear mushroom and spring onion, 30-50 CNY at restaurants); you mian kao lao lao (莜面栲栳栳, steamed rolled oatmeal tubes dipped in mutton sauce, a rustic mountain staple). In Doucun: one family-run noodle shop near the village entrance serves simple dao xiao mian (15 CNY) and liang pi (cold noodles). Bring packed lunch from Taiyuan for the temple visit. Taiyuan: Taiyuan Mianshi Guan (太原面食馆) on Jiefang Road serves 50+ noodle varieties -- allow 60-80 CNY/person.",
"se":"April, May, September, October are ideal -- daytime 15-25C, clear mountain air, dry. The temple courtyard gets golden afternoon light around 3-4pm. June-August: 25-35C, afternoon thunderstorms possible, road is fine but humid. November-March: temple open but mountain road may close after snow; daytime 0-10C; temple caretaker may reduce hours. The temple opens 8:30am-5:30pm year-round. Chinese New Year (late Jan/Feb): temple may close for 1-3 days -- call ahead. Golden Week (Oct 1-7): surprisingly manageable here -- too remote for mass tourism. Visit early morning for best light and solitude.",
"ti":"CRITICAL: No English signage anywhere in the temple or surrounding area. Download Pleco (Chinese dictionary app) and Baidu Translate offline pack before the trip. Google Maps does NOT work reliably in China -- use Baidu Maps or Amap (高德地图). Your driver can navigate but you'll need the temple name in Chinese: 佛光寺东大殿. Bring CASH -- no credit cards, no WeChat Pay for foreigners without Chinese bank account. Bring at least 200 CNY per person. Altitude is 1,500m -- bring a light jacket even in summer; mountain evenings are cool. The toilets are primitive squat toilets -- bring your own toilet paper and hand sanitizer. Photography is permitted in the courtyard but flash is prohibited inside the hall. Combine with Nanchan Temple (30 min drive) -- they are on the same mountain road. A driver will charge 800 CNY total for both temples in one day."
},
"nanchan":{
"t":"Same access as Foguang Temple -- both temples are on the same mountain road in Wutai County, 30 minutes apart by car. Fly into Taiyuan Wusu Airport (TYN). Hire private car+driver from Taiyuan (600-800 CNY/day). Drive 4h northeast. The road from Doucun to Nanchan branches off the main S310 onto a narrower village lane -- the last 500m is a steep paved path; park at the base and walk 10 min uphill to the temple gate. NO public transit. NO bus. Your driver must wait (there is nothing at the parking area -- no shops, no shelter).",
"s":"Same as Foguang -- day-trip from Taiyuan is the standard approach. Doucun farmhouse (80-120 CNY, very basic, shared squat toilet, no shower in some rooms). The Doucun guesthouses serve simple dinner (20-30 CNY per person, home-cooked Shanxi noodles and stir-fry). If you stay overnight in Doucun, bring earplugs -- roosters start at 5am. In Taiyuan: Pullman Taiyuan (500 CNY, best overall); Hanting Hotel near Taiyuan Railway Station (160 CNY, clean budget). Tip: book a hotel near Taiyuan Railway Station or Wuyi Square for easy morning departure.",
"f":"Doucun village has exactly 2 eateries: one noodle shop and one small grocery store selling bottled water and packaged snacks. The noodle shop serves dao xiao mian (15 CNY) and simple stir-fried vegetables with rice (20 CNY). Bring your own lunch from Taiyuan -- there are no restaurants at the temple itself. Taiyuan food streets to stock up: Liuxiang (柳巷) pedestrian street has bakeries, fruit vendors, and snack shops. Buy bottled water, fruit, and packaged bread/cakes in Taiyuan the night before. Local specialty to try: pingyao niu rou (平遥牛肉, Pingyao spiced beef -- available vacuum-packed in Taiyuan supermarkets as travel snack).",
"se":"Same mountain valley as Foguang, identical climate. April, May, September, October: best months, 15-25C, dry and clear. The temple faces south and gets beautiful morning light (8-10am is the golden window for photos). June-August: hot but visitable; afternoon rain possible. Winter: Nov-Mar the road is often icy; temple may close without notice during heavy snow. Hours: 8:30am-5:30pm. Unlike Foguang, Nanchan gets almost no visitors -- you will likely have the entire temple to yourself regardless of season. The quiet is part of the experience.",
"ti":"Nanchan is modest -- a small three-bay hall, not a temple complex. Manage expectations. The magic is knowing you are standing inside the oldest wooden building in China (782 AD) when Charlemagne ruled Europe. The temple guardian (an elderly local man) may give an impromptu tour in heavy Shanxi dialect -- tip 20-30 CNY if he does. No English. No gift shop. No postcards. No card machines. Cash only -- bring small notes (10, 20 CNY). The guardian may not have change for 100 CNY notes. Photography: permitted outside, flash prohibited inside the hall. Combine with Foguang in one day -- they are 30 min apart. Driver: negotiate 800 CNY for the full-day mountain loop (Taiyuan-Foguang-Nanchan-Taiyuan) -- this is the standard rate."
},

"dayanta":{
"t":"Fly into Xi'an Xianyang Airport (XIY). Airport shuttle bus (25 CNY, 60 min) or taxi (120-150 CNY, 40 min) to city center. The pagoda is in Yanta District, south of the city wall. Metro Line 3 to Dayanta Station (大雁塔站), Exit B -- walk 5 min north. Metro Line 4 also serves Dayanta area (Datang Furongyuan Station is 10 min walk). Buses 5, 21, 22, 23, 24, 30, 41, 224, 500, 601 all stop at Dayanta. From Bell Tower area: 20 min by taxi (25 CNY). From Xi'an Railway Station: Metro Line 1 to Wulukou, transfer to Line 3. The pagoda is the most central major attraction in Xi'an -- you cannot miss it.",
"s":"Yanta District has Xi'an's best hotel cluster. Luxury: Wyndham Grand Xi'an South (5 min walk, 500-700 CNY/night, Western breakfast, English staff, views of pagoda). Mid-range: Eastern House Boutique Hotel (10 min walk, 350-450 CNY/night, converted traditional courtyard, excellent breakfast). Budget: Xi'an Xiangzimen Youth Hostel near South Gate (60 CNY/dorm, 180 CNY/private, 20 min by metro, best social hostel in Xi'an). Also: Hanting Hotel Dayanta (200 CNY/night, 5 min walk, clean chain hotel). All hotels in this area can book through Trip.com, Booking.com, or Agoda.",
"f":"Xi'an is arguably China's best food city. Near the pagoda: the area around Yanta West Road and Cuihua Road has dozens of restaurants. Must-try: rou jia mo (肉夹馍, minced pork in baked flatbread, Xi'an's 'Chinese burger', 10-15 CNY from street stalls); yang rou pao mo (羊肉泡馍, lamb soup with hand-torn flatbread, 30-50 CNY at a restaurant -- go to Yishenlou 义盛楼 near Bell Tower); liang pi (凉皮, cold wheat noodles in chili-vinegar sauce, 8-12 CNY). The Muslim Quarter (回民街 Huimin Jie, 20 min by taxi north) is Xi'an's most famous food street -- 200+ stalls serving lamb skewers, persimmon cakes, pomegranate juice. Go in the evening for the full atmosphere. For upscale: First Noodle Under the Sun (天下第一面) near Dayanta serves biang biang mian (belt noodles) -- 60-80 CNY/person.",
"se":"April, May, September, October: 15-28C, comfortable. The pagoda opens 8am-5:30pm. Best photos: early morning (8-9am) when tour groups haven't arrived, or sunset (5-6pm) when the pagoda catches golden light. Summer (June-August): 30-40C, very crowded with domestic tourists; go at 8am or after 4pm. Winter (Nov-Feb): 0-10C, the pagoda looks striking with snow on the eaves, much fewer visitors. The North Square fountain show runs nightly at 8:30pm (free, 20 min) -- impressive but crowded. Chinese New Year: special lantern displays. Golden Week (Oct 1-7): extremely crowded, avoid if possible.",
"ti":"IMPORTANT: This is a Ming dynasty (1500s) rebuild on a Tang foundation. NOT a Tang original structure. The pagoda is 64m tall, 7 floors, with a narrow interior staircase (no elevator). Climbing takes 10-15 min. The combo ticket (pagoda + Daci'en Temple complex, 50 CNY) is worth it. Book Shaanxi History Museum (free, 1km south) 3-5 days ahead via WeChat mini-program -- it has the best Tang artifacts anywhere. The museum fills quotas daily; book for 8:30am slot. For foreigners: download Alipay and link your international credit card before arriving in China -- it works for most purchases including museum bookings. The Dayanta area is very walkable -- you can combine pagoda + museum + Great Tang All Day Mall (1km south) in one day."
},
"mogao":{
"t":"Fly into Dunhuang Mogao Airport (DNH) -- direct flights from Lanzhou (1.5h), Xi'an (2.5h), Beijing (4h). From airport: taxi to Dunhuang city center (13km, 30 min, 50 CNY). The Mogao Caves Digital Exhibition Center (莫高窟数字展示中心) is 9km southeast of Dunhuang city. Take the green city shuttle bus (line 绿皮专线) from outside Dunhuang Hotel or the bus station -- 20 CNY, departs every 30 min, 30 min ride. Taxi from Dunhuang city center: 60 CNY one way. NOTE: you CANNOT drive directly to the caves. All visitors must go to the Digital Center first, watch two orientation films (40 min total), then take the park shuttle bus (included in ticket) 15km to the actual caves.",
"s":"Dunhuang city is compact and all hotels are within 10 min of the shuttle bus. Best options: Silk Road Dunhuang Hotel (敦煌丝路酒店, 4-star, 400-500 CNY/night, Silk Road-themed decor, 5 min to shuttle, best overall); Dunhuang Mountain Villa (敦煌山庄, 600-800 CNY/night, luxury desert-resort style, excellent restaurant); Dunhuang Yunfei International Youth Hostel (敦煌云飞国际青年旅舍, 50 CNY/dorm, 150 CNY/private, popular backpacker spot, arranges desert camping tours). Book 2-3 weeks ahead during peak season (May-Oct). Some hotels can help book Mogao tickets -- ask when reserving.",
"f":"Dunhuang food reflects its Silk Road crossroads location -- a mix of Chinese, Central Asian, and Muslim influences. Must-try: Lanzhou beef noodles (兰州牛肉面, 15-20 CNY, the definitive northwest Chinese breakfast -- best at Ma Zilu Beef Noodles 马子禄牛肉面 near the night market); mutton skewers (羊肉串, 3-5 CNY per skewer, grilled over charcoal with cumin and chili, best at Shazhou Night Market after 7pm); Dunhuang huang mian (敦煌黄面, yellow wheat noodles in lamb broth, local specialty, 20-30 CNY); Dunhuang xing pi cha (杏皮茶, local apricot tea, cold, sweet, 5 CNY per cup, sold everywhere). The Shazhou Night Market (沙洲夜市) is the culinary heart of Dunhuang -- 100+ food stalls, open 6pm-midnight. Try the dried fruits and melons -- Dunhuang melons are legendary (July-Sept).",
"se":"Spring (April-May): 10-25C, occasional sandstorms (bring sunglasses and a scarf). This is the best period -- pleasant temperatures, clear skies. Autumn (September-October): 10-25C, also excellent. Summer (June-August): 25-40C, very hot midday but the caves stay cool (naturally climate-controlled by the cliff face). Go at the earliest slot (8:30am). Winter (November-March): -10 to 5C, some caves close for conservation rotation, far fewer visitors. The Mogao Caves operate year-round with reduced capacity in winter. First morning slot (8:30am) has the best light quality for looking at murals -- the low-angle sun brings out colors. Book Standard Visit tickets (8 caves, 238 CNY) 2-3 weeks ahead via the official WeChat mini-program (search 莫高窟参观预约). English guide tours available -- request when booking.",
"ti":"This is the single greatest body of Tang pictorial art on earth -- 45,000 sq meters of murals across 492 caves. The Standard Visit shows 8 caves (including the famous Cave 96 with the 35m Buddha, Cave 16-17 Library Cave, and Cave 45 with the best Tang sculptures). Tours are strictly guided, 25 people per group, timed at 75-90 minutes. NO photography of any kind inside caves -- phone cameras included. The flash destroys pigments. Before the caves: the Digital Center shows two excellent 20-min films -- the first on Silk Road history, the second a 360-degree virtual cave tour. After the caves: visit the Mogao Caves Exhibition Center (free, near the cave exit) for replica caves you CAN photograph. Combine with Singing Sand Dunes & Crescent Moon Spring (鸣沙山月牙泉, 5km from city, 120 CNY, go at sunrise or sunset) and Yumen Pass (玉门关, 90km northwest, the ancient Han Dynasty Silk Road gate)."
},

"famen":{
"t":"Famen Temple (法门寺) is 120km west of Xi'an in Fufeng County, Baoji City. From Xi'an: take HSR from Xi'an North Station (西安北站) to Baoji South Station (宝鸡南站) -- 1 hour, 60 CNY, frequent departures every 30 min. From Baoji South, taxi 30 min to Famen Temple (40 CNY). Alternative: direct bus from Xi'an West Bus Station (城西客运站) to Famen Temple -- 2.5 hours, 35 CNY, departures at 8am and 9am daily. Private car from Xi'an: 2-2.5 hours via G30 expressway, 400 CNY return with driver waiting. The temple is well-signposted from the expressway exit. Day trip from Xi'an is very manageable -- leave at 7am, return by 4pm.",
"s":"Most visitors day-trip from Xi'an -- no need to stay overnight near the temple. Baoji city (25km from Famen): Crowne Plaza Baoji (400-500 CNY/night, best hotel in Baoji, English-speaking staff); Baoji Marriott (350-450 CNY/night). Famen town: Famen Temple Hotel (法门寺酒店, 200 CNY/night, basic but walking distance to temple, hot water, Chinese only). Best strategy: stay in Xi'an, day-trip to Famen. If you want to explore more of Baoji area (Bronze Ware Museum, Taibai Mountain), stay 1-2 nights in Baoji.",
"f":"Baoji cuisine is classic Shaanxi with its own twist. Must-try: saozi mian (臊子面, minced pork and vegetable noodle soup, the Qishan county specialty, 15-20 CNY); gan mian pi (擀面皮, cold flat noodles with chili oil, the quintessential Baoji street food, 8-12 CNY); rou jia mo (腊汁肉夹馍, Fufeng style is slightly sweeter than Xi'an, 10-15 CNY). The Famen Temple complex has a Buddhist vegetarian restaurant (40 CNY/person, buffet style, attached to the temple). The food is simple but authentic -- monks eat here. In Baoji city: Lao Cheng Yi Wan Mian (老城一碗面) near the train station serves Qishan saozi mian and other local noodles, 30-50 CNY/person.",
"se":"April, May, September, October: best weather, 15-25C. The museum is indoor and climate-controlled so the experience is good year-round. Summer (June-August): 30-38C outside but the underground museum and galleries are air-conditioned. Winter (November-February): 0-10C, much fewer visitors, atmospheric with winter light on the pagoda. Hours: 8:30am-5:30pm (last entry 4:30pm). Arrive at opening (8:30am) -- the underground crypt has a timed entry system and morning groups are smaller. Chinese New Year: temple holds a large prayer festival, very crowded but culturally interesting. Avoid Golden Week (Oct 1-7): domestic tourist crush.",
"ti":"CRITICAL: The above-ground temple complex is a massive, modern, and not-subtle construction built in the 2000s. It is architecturally questionable. Ignore it. What matters: (1) The underground museum -- this is where the Tang crypt was discovered in 1987. You walk through the actual excavation site. (2) The Treasure Gallery -- houses the Tang gold, silver, glass, and textile artifacts. The four nested Buddhist reliquary sets (gold-silver-gold-stone caskets containing the Buddha finger bone sarira) are displayed here. The famous four-faced Buddha staff (四股十二环锡杖) is a highlight. English labels are minimal in most galleries -- download the Smart Famen app or hire an English-speaking guide at the museum entrance (200 CNY, 1.5 hours, worth it). Photography is permitted in the museum (no flash). The sacred finger bone relic itself is displayed in a special chapel with controlled lighting -- no photos in this room."
},
"daming":{
"t":"Daming Palace is in northern Xi'an, 3km north of the Bell Tower and Xi'an city wall. Metro Line 2 to Longshouyuan Station (龙首原站), Exit D -- walk 10 min south to the Danfeng Gate (south entrance). Metro Line 4 to Daming Palace West Station (大明宫西站) for the west entrance. City buses 2, 17, 22, 46, 104, 309 all stop at Daming Palace South Gate. Taxi from Bell Tower area: 20-25 CNY, 15 min. The park is enormous (3.2 sq km) -- the Danfeng Gate is the main entrance. Rent a bike (20 CNY/hour) at the south entrance if you want to cover the full site.",
"s":"The park area sits between the old city wall and the new northern district. Hilton Xi'an (600-700 CNY/night, 10 min walk to Daming Palace south gate, elevated views of the park, best upscale choice); Xi'an Bell Tower Hotel (300-400 CNY/night, 30 min walk or 10 min taxi, in the heart of old city); Jinjiang Inn Xi'an North Station branch (150-200 CNY/night, 2 metro stops north, clean budget). The area immediately north of the park (Fengcheng Road corridor) has newer business hotels: Atour Hotel Fengcheng 5th Road (300-350 CNY/night, stylish mid-range Chinese chain, popular with young travelers).",
"f":"The area north of Daming Palace is a newer residential district with fewer historic restaurants. Best option: eat near the Bell Tower area before or after visiting, then take the 15-min taxi north. Near the park: Taihua Road has a small food street with noodle shops and BBQ stalls; try yang rou chuan (羊肉串, lamb skewers) from the Uyghur-style BBQ stalls. Defachang Dumpling House (德发长饺子馆) near Bell Tower -- Xi'an's most famous dumpling restaurant since 1936, serves a dumpling banquet (饺子宴, 100-200 CNY/person, 20+ types of dumplings). Bring water and snacks into the park -- there is ONE small kiosk near the Danfeng Gate and that's it for the entire 3.2 sq km park.",
"se":"April, May, September, October: 15-25C, comfortable for walking this enormous open-air park. Early morning (8-9:30am) is the best time -- soft light on the grass-covered foundations. Summer (June-August): 30-40C, very hot with almost no shade -- the park is mostly open grass. Go at 8am or after 5pm. Winter: park open but biting wind across the open space. Spring is surprisingly beautiful -- the western section has cherry blossoms (late March-early April). Hours: 8am-6pm (summer), 8:30am-5:30pm (winter). Danfeng Gate Museum hours same as park. The park is free to enter; the core archaeological zone and museum charge 60 CNY.",
"ti":"MANAGE EXPECTATIONS: This is an archaeological park, not a standing palace. The Tang buildings are gone. You walk across grass-covered building foundations -- slightly raised earth platforms marking where halls once stood. The scale is the experience -- Daming Palace was 4.5 times larger than the Forbidden City. The reconstructed Danfeng Gate (south entrance, red, massive) houses a small museum with site models and Tang artifacts. The museum has English audio guides (40 CNY rental). The Hanyuan Hall foundations (the main audience hall, northern area) are the most impressive -- raised platform 15m high with views across the park. Rent a bike to cover the full site (2-3 hours walking, 1 hour cycling). After visiting: walk south through the old city to the Xi'an City Wall (20 min walk/10 min taxi) for the afternoon."
},
"datangbuye":{
"t":"Great Tang All Day Mall (大唐不夜城) is in southern Xi'an's Yanta District. Metro Line 3 to Dayanta Station (大雁塔站), Exit C -- you emerge directly at the north end of the pedestrian street. Metro Line 4 to Datang Furongyuan Station (大唐芙蓉园站) for the south end. Buses 5, 21, 22, 23, 24, 30, 224, 500, 601 all stop nearby. From Bell Tower: taxi 25 min (25 CNY) or Metro Line 2 to Xiaozhai, transfer to Line 3. The street runs north-south for 2.1km from Big Wild Goose Pagoda (north) to Kaiyuan Square (south). It is pedestrian-only after 4pm.",
"s":"This is Xi'an's most modern district -- excellent hotel cluster. Wyndham Grand Xi'an South (5 min walk, 500-700 CNY, views of the street from upper floors, best location); Hilton Xi'an High-tech Zone (15 min walk north, 450-550 CNY); Atour Hotel Dayanta (300-400 CNY, 5 min walk, stylish Chinese boutique chain). For the full Tang-themed experience: Tang Dynasty Hotel (唐代酒店, 400 CNY, Tang-style decor, but very touristy). Budget: Hanting Hotel Dayanta (200 CNY, clean, 5 min walk). The street is active until midnight -- staying nearby means you can walk back to your hotel after the lights show.",
"f":"The street itself has Tang-themed food stalls selling tanghulu (糖葫芦, candied hawthorn skewers, 10 CNY), grilled squid, and pearl milk tea. These are overpriced tourist fare (20-40 CNY per item). For real food: walk 5 min east to Yanta West Road (雁塔西路) and Cuihua Road (翠华路) -- these streets have dozens of authentic local restaurants. Must-try in this area: Yangzhou fried rice at a local stir-fry shop (15 CNY); malatang (麻辣烫, choose-your-own-ingredients spicy hotpot, 25-40 CNY); and Xi'an Dishui Cave Food Street (西安滴水洞食街, 5 min walk east) -- a covered food hall with 50+ stalls. For a splurge: Tang Palace (唐宫) on Yanta West Road serves imperial Tang-style banquet food, 200-300 CNY/person.",
"se":"This is a NIGHTTIME attraction. The street is unremarkable during the day -- go after 6:30pm (winter) or 7:30pm (summer) when the Tang-style lanterns and building lights come on. The light shows, street performances (Tang dancing, poetry recitals, acrobatics), and atmosphere peak between 7:30-9:30pm. Spring (April-May) and autumn (September-October) evenings are most comfortable (15-22C). Summer nights are hot (25-32C) but the atmosphere is at maximum -- hundreds of visitors in rented Tang costumes doing photoshoots. Winter: 0-5C evenings, far fewer visitors, the lanterns look beautiful in the cold clear air. Rainy nights: the street empties but the wet pavement reflects the lights beautifully -- perfect for photos. The street is open year-round, free admission.",
"ti":"LET US BE HONEST: This is a 2010 theme park built for tourism and commerce. Nothing here is historic. It exists because Xi'an wanted a Tang-themed commercial district. That said: the light displays are genuinely impressive, the Tang-style architecture (while modern) gives a sense of Tang aesthetic, and the atmosphere is fun. The street is 2.1km long -- wear comfortable shoes. You will see hundreds of visitors in rented Tang dynasty costumes (Hanfu, 汉服) taking photos -- costume rental shops line the side streets, 100-200 CNY including hair and makeup. If you want: rent a costume and join them, it's part of the experience. BUT if you came to Xi'an for genuine history, spend your morning at Shaanxi History Museum, afternoon at Daming Palace or the City Wall, and come here only in the evening as atmospheric entertainment. Combine with Big Wild Goose Pagoda (walk 5 min north) -- see the pagoda at sunset, then walk south into the mall for the evening."
},

"baoguo":{
"t":"Baoguo Temple (保国寺) is 15km north of Ningbo city center in Hongtang Town (洪塘镇). Fly into Ningbo Lishe Airport (NGB, 15km south of city) or fly into Shanghai (PVG/SHA) and take HSR. From Shanghai: HSR from Shanghai Hongqiao to Ningbo (2 hours, 140 CNY, departures every 30 min). From Hangzhou: HSR to Ningbo (1 hour, 80 CNY). From Ningbo Railway Station: Bus 238 to Baoguo Temple stop (40 min, 2 CNY). Taxi from Ningbo city center/Tianyi Square: 30 min (50 CNY). The temple entrance is on a hillside -- a short uphill walk from the bus stop. Ningbo Metro Line 2 to Lucheng Station, then taxi 10 min (15 CNY).",
"s":"Stay in Ningbo city center -- the temple area is suburban with no nearby hotels. Ningbo Marriott Hotel (600 CNY/night, riverfront Old Bund location, best upscale); Crowne Plaza City Center (500 CNY/night, near Tianyi Square shopping area); Home Inn Ningbo Railway Station (180 CNY/night, reliable budget chain). For a local experience: Flower Blooms Boutique Hotel near Moon Lake (350 CNY/night, converted traditional house). Old Bund (Laowaitan) area is the nicest place to stay -- riverside promenade with bars and restaurants.",
"f":"Ningbo cuisine (宁波菜) is one of China's great regional cooking styles, focused on fresh seafood and delicate flavors. Must-try: Ningbo tang yuan (宁波汤圆, glutinous rice balls filled with black sesame paste in sweet osmanthus soup, 15-25 CNY -- best at Gang Ya Gou 缸鸭狗 near Tianyi Square, a famous shop since 1926); yellow croaker in soy sauce (雪菜大黄鱼, Ningbo's signature fish dish, 60-100 CNY at seafood restaurants); xie chao nian gao (蟹炒年糕, wok-fried rice cakes with crab, 40-60 CNY). The Old Bund (Laowaitan, 老外滩) riverside has dozens of restaurants -- Chamate (慈舍) serves elegant Ningbo cuisine in a heritage building (100-150 CNY/person). Near the temple: Hongtang town has simple noodle shops serving Ningbo-style seafood noodles (15-25 CNY).",
"se":"Spring (March-May): 12-25C, cherry blossoms in the temple courtyard (late March), best season. Autumn (October-November): 15-22C, clear skies, persimmons ripen on temple trees. Avoid mid-June to early July -- the Meiyu (plum rain) season brings 3-4 weeks of continuous rain and 85%+ humidity, very uncomfortable. Summer (July-August): 28-38C, humid, visitable but sweaty. Winter (December-February): 5-10C, damp cold. The temple is rarely crowded in any season -- it is overlooked by domestic tourists who focus on Tianyi Pavilion and the Bund. Hours: 8am-4:30pm. Admission: 20 CNY.",
"ti":"Only the Main Hall (Dadian) is Song dynasty (1013) -- it is the oldest surviving wooden building in southern China. Other halls in the complex are Ming-Qing reconstructions. The Main Hall's dougong (斗拱) bracket system predates the official Song building code Yingzao Fashi (营造法式, 1103) -- this makes it the earliest datable example of mature Chinese timber-frame architecture. The temple is virtually unknown to foreign tourists -- you will likely be alone here, which is a rare luxury at a national treasure site in China. The temple caretakers may offer to show you hidden details (Buddhist carvings on ceiling beams) for a small tip. After the temple: combine with Tianyi Pavilion (天一阁, 30 min by taxi, China's oldest private library from 1561, 30 CNY) and Ningbo Museum (宁波博物馆, designed by Pritzker winner Wang Shu, free, stunning building made of recycled bricks and tiles -- 20 min by taxi)."
},
"huqiu":{
"t":"Tiger Hill (虎丘) is in northwest Suzhou, 5km from the old city center. Fly into Shanghai Hongqiao (SHA, 80km east) or Shanghai Pudong (PVG, 120km east). From Shanghai: HSR from Shanghai Station or Hongqiao to Suzhou Station (25 min, 40 CNY, trains every 10-15 min -- one of China's most frequent HSR routes). From Suzhou Railway Station: Tourist Bus Line 1 (游1路) or Line 2 (游2路) directly to Tiger Hill (30 min, 2 CNY). Taxi from Suzhou Station: 20 min (30 CNY). Suzhou Metro Line 1 to Yangyu Xiang Station (养育巷站), then bus or taxi 10 min. The hill is a large landscaped park -- the pagoda sits at the summit, visible from the entrance.",
"s":"Suzhou's best hotels are in the old city (Gusu district) or near the gardens. Pan Pacific Suzhou (600 CNY/night, 5 min drive to Tiger Hill, set in a traditional garden, excellent breakfast); Suzhou Hantang Inn (250-350 CNY/night, converted Qing dynasty guildhall near Shantang Street, atmospheric); Pingjiang Lodge (400 CNY/night, on Pingjiang Road canal street, traditional Suzhou courtyard house, stunning). Budget: Suzhou Mingtown Youth Hostel near Guanqian Street (60 CNY/dorm, 200 CNY/private room, in a converted traditional residence). The Shantang Street (山塘街) area is the most atmospheric place to stay -- historic canal street with tea houses.",
"f":"Suzhou cuisine is famous for its subtle sweetness and meticulous presentation. Must-try: song shu gui yu (松鼠桂鱼, squirrel-shaped deep-fried mandarin fish in sweet-sour sauce, Suzhou's iconic dish, 120-180 CNY at proper restaurants -- go to Song He Lou 松鹤楼 on Guanqian Street, operating since 1757); xie fen xiaolong (蟹粉小笼, crab roe soup dumplings, 30-50 CNY per steamer -- best at Tongdexing 同得兴); mi zhi ou pian (蜜汁藕片, lotus root stuffed with sticky rice in osmanthus syrup, 25 CNY). Shantang Street (山塘街, 10 min walk from Tiger Hill) is the best food destination near the hill -- canal-side restaurants serving Suzhou home-style dishes. For a splurge: Xinsongyuan (新松园) near the Panmen Gate serves imperial Suzhou banquet cuisine (300-500 CNY/person tasting menu).",
"se":"Spring (March-May): 15-25C, Suzhou's gardens burst into bloom -- this is Suzhou at its most beautiful. Plum blossoms in March, cherry and magnolia in late March-early April, wisteria in May. Autumn (September-November): 15-22C, osmanthus fragrance fills the air, the hill's maple trees turn red. Summer (June-August): 28-38C, extremely humid, stick to morning visits. Winter: 0-10C, the pagoda in winter mist is atmospheric -- fewer tourists. Photographers: the pagoda catches best light at 8-10am (east-facing). AVOID Golden Week (Oct 1-7) and any Chinese national holiday -- Tiger Hill becomes shoulder-to-shoulder crowded. Hours: 7:30am-5:30pm (summer), 7:30am-5pm (winter). Admission: 60 CNY (winter), 80 CNY (summer).",
"ti":"The 3-degree lean of the pagoda is visible to the naked eye -- stand at the south-east corner for the best viewing angle. You CANNOT enter the pagoda -- it has been closed to visitors since the 1980s due to structural concerns. The hill is not just the pagoda: explore the Sword Pool (剑池, allegedly where King He Lu of Wu buried 3,000 swords in 496 BC), the Thousand People Rock (千人石), and the Wanjing Villa bonsai garden near the exit (free with ticket). Tiger Hill tea house serves local Biluochun green tea (碧螺春, one of China's top ten teas -- grown on Dongting Mountain near Suzhou). Combine with: Suzhou Museum (3km southeast, designed by I.M. Pei, free but book 3-7 days ahead via Suzhou Museum app -- this is a must-see, the building itself is a masterpiece), Humble Administrator's Garden (拙政园, 2km from Tiger Hill, 80 CNY, largest Suzhou garden), and Shantang Street (walk there from Tiger Hill, 10 min, the best-preserved canal street in Suzhou)."
},
"kaifengtieta":{
"t":"Kaifeng Iron Pagoda (开封铁塔) is in the northeast corner of Kaifeng's old city. Fly into Zhengzhou Xinzheng International Airport (CGO, 70km south of Kaifeng). From airport: direct shuttle bus to Kaifeng (1.5 hours, 40 CNY) or HSR from Zhengzhou East Station to Kaifeng North Station (20 min, 25 CNY, every 20 min). From Kaifeng North HSR Station: taxi 20 min to Iron Pagoda (25 CNY). From Kaifeng Railway Station: Bus 1, 3, 4, or 20 to Iron Pagoda Park (铁塔公园). Taxi from anywhere in Kaifeng: 15-20 CNY. The pagoda is inside Iron Pagoda Park -- you'll see it rising above the park wall as you approach.",
"s":"Kaifeng is budget-friendly. Kaifeng Guesthouse Hotel (350 CNY/night, inside Qingming Riverside Garden area, Song-themed rooms); Pullman Kaifeng (450 CNY/night, near Dragon Pavilion/Long Ting scenic area, international standard, best in town); 7 Days Inn Kaifeng Drum Tower (130 CNY/night, near the night market, basic but clean). Kaifeng has no luxury international hotels -- the Pullman is the top option. For a unique stay: the area around the Drum Tower (鼓楼) has converted courtyard guesthouses -- Qingming Shanghe Yuan Hotel (300 CNY).",
"f":"Kaifeng is one of China's great night-market food cities. Must-try: guan tang bao (灌汤包, soup-filled steamed buns, Kaifeng's signature dish -- the skin is so thin you drink the soup through a straw, 25-40 CNY per basket, best at Kaifeng Diyilou 开封第一楼 since 1922); tong zi ji (桶子鸡, whole young chicken braised in spiced broth then chilled and sliced, 50-80 CNY); hua sheng gao (花生糕, Kaifeng peanut cake, 10 CNY per box, sold everywhere in the night market); xing ren cha (杏仁茶, hot almond tea with dried fruit, 8-15 CNY, street stalls). The Drum Tower Night Market (鼓楼夜市, Gulou Yeshi) is Kaifeng's culinary heart -- opens 6pm, runs until midnight, 100+ food stalls in a block-wide pedestrian square. Also: Xisi Night Market (西司夜市) near Baogong Lake -- less touristy than Drum Tower, more local clientele.",
"se":"Spring (April-May): 15-25C, comfortable, the park's gardens bloom. Autumn (September-October): 12-24C, clear skies, Kaifeng Chrysanthemum Festival (mid-October to mid-November) fills the park with elaborate chrysanthemum displays -- the best time to visit. Summer (June-August): 28-38C, very humid (Kaifeng sits on the Yellow River floodplain), visit early morning. Winter: 0-8C, dry and grey, the brown-glazed pagoda stands stark against a Henan winter sky -- minimalist beauty. Hours: 8am-5:30pm (summer), 8am-5pm (winter). Admission: 40 CNY.",
"ti":"This is the ONLY Song dynasty structure still above ground in Kaifeng. All of Northern Song Kaifeng -- the million-person capital of the world's richest empire -- lies buried 8 meters under Yellow River silt, deposited by centuries of floods. This pagoda survived because it was built on a small hill. The pagoda is 55.9 meters tall, 13 stories, and built of brown-glazed brick (NOT iron -- the name comes from the iron-like color). You CAN climb the interior staircase (steep, narrow, no handrails on some sections -- not recommended for claustrophobic or elderly visitors). The 360-degree view from upper windows shows the Yellow River to the north and modern Kaifeng sprawling below. The park surrounding the pagoda is pleasant but unremarkable. First: visit Kaifeng Museum (开封博物馆, free, 1 hour) to understand what Song Kaifeng looked like -- it has excellent diorama models. Then: Qingming Riverside Garden (清明上河园, 1998 theme park, 120 CNY) is 20 min by taxi on the other side of the old city -- go if you want the theme park experience, skip if you only want genuine history."
},

"jinci":{
"t":"Jinci Temple (晋祠) is 25km southwest of Taiyuan city center. Fly into Taiyuan Wusu Airport (TYN, 15km southeast of city). From Taiyuan: Bus 308 from Taiyuan Railway Station to Jinci Gongyuan (晋祠公园) -- 60 min, 2 CNY, departs every 15 min. Bus 804 from Taiyuan South Railway Station (太原南站) -- also 60 min, 2 CNY. Taxi from Taiyuan city center: 40 min (60-80 CNY). From Taiyuan South HSR Station: 35 min by taxi (50 CNY). The temple complex is easy to find -- the main gate faces the main road and the park entrance is large and well-marked. Free electric carts shuttle visitors from the outer park gate to the temple entrance (500m).",
"s":"Stay in Taiyuan city. Pullman Taiyuan (500 CNY/night, near Wuyi Square and Liuxiang shopping street, best upscale); Taiyuan Wanda Realm (400 CNY/night, connected to a large shopping mall with restaurants); Jinjiang Inn Taiyuan Fu Xi Jie (150 CNY/night, clean budget chain, 10 min walk to Food Street). The temple area has no hotels -- it is a suburban park setting. Plan a morning departure (8am) from your Taiyuan hotel, visit the temple (2-3 hours), return to Taiyuan for lunch. If you are visiting Foguang and Nanchan temples (the remote Tang sites), use Taiyuan as your base for 2-3 days.",
"f":"Taiyuan is the noodle capital of Shanxi. Must-try: dao xiao mian (刀削面, knife-shaved noodles -- the cook holds a block of dough and shaves noodles directly into boiling water with a curved blade, 15-20 CNY at any noodle shop); guo you rou (过油肉, oil-braised sliced pork with black wood ear mushrooms, the classic Shanxi restaurant dish, 35-50 CNY); yang za ge (羊杂割, lamb offal soup with vermicelli noodles, a Taiyuan breakfast staple, 15-20 CNY at morning street stalls); lao chen cu (老陈醋, aged Shanxi vinegar -- visit the Donghu Vinegar Culture Museum 东湖醋园 near the railway station, free, fascinating tour of 600-year-old vinegar vats, 20 min). Taiyuan Mianshi Guan (太原面食馆) on Jiefang Road -- order the noodle tasting platter (面食套餐) with 8-10 different noodle types, 60-80 CNY/person. Food Street (食品街 Shipin Jie) near Liuxiang has 50+ restaurants and food stalls.",
"se":"Spring (April-May): 12-25C, peach and plum blossoms in the temple gardens -- this is the most beautiful season, especially late April when the Nanlao Spring-fed ponds reflect pink blossoms. Autumn (September-October): 10-22C, ancient cypress trees contrast with autumn foliage. Summer (June-August): 25-35C, but the temple complex is shaded by 1,000+ year old cypress trees (some planted in the Zhou Dynasty, now 3,000+ years old and still alive) -- surprisingly cool. Winter: -5 to 5C, the temple with snow is serene but cold. Hours: 8am-5:30pm (summer), 8:30am-5pm (winter). Admission: 80 CNY (peak season), 60 CNY (off-peak). Go early (8-9am) to experience the temple before tour groups arrive from Taiyuan.",
"ti":"The Hall of the Holy Mother (Shengmudian, 圣母殿) is the jewel -- built 1032, it is the earliest surviving major Song dynasty timber hall. The eight carved wooden dragons wrapping around the front columns are UNIQUE in surviving Chinese architecture -- no other building from any period has this feature. They are not separate pieces but carved into the columns themselves. Also: the Nanlao Spring (难老泉), one of China's most celebrated springs, feeds a series of pools through the temple complex; the Zhou Dynasty Cypress (周柏, 3,000+ years old, leaning at 45 degrees against a younger tree); the 40 life-size painted clay statues of palace ladies inside the Hall of Holy Mother (Song dynasty originals). The temple complex has many Ming-Qing additions -- focus your time on the Song core (Hall of Holy Mother, Flying Bridge, and Offerings Hall). Combine with: Shanxi Museum (山西博物院) in Taiyuan (free, 2 hours, excellent Neolithic-to-Tang exhibits that provide context for Jinci), and if time: Shuangta Temple (双塔寺, Twin Pagodas, Ming dynasty, 30 CNY, in southeastern Taiyuan)."
},
"longxing":{
"t":"Longxing Temple (隆兴寺) is in Zhengding Ancient Town (正定古城), 15km north of Shijiazhuang city center. Fly into Beijing Capital Airport (PEK, 280km northeast) or Shijiazhuang Zhengding International Airport (SJW, 20km north of Zhengding). From Beijing: HSR from Beijing West Station to Shijiazhuang Station (1 hour, 130 CNY, frequent departures). From Shijiazhuang Railway Station: Bus 177 to Zhengding Longxing Temple stop (50 min, 3 CNY). Taxi from Shijiazhuang city center: 30 min (40 CNY). From Shijiazhuang Zhengding Airport: taxi 20 min (30 CNY). The temple is in the center of Zhengding old town -- the main gate faces a large square and the approach street is lined with Ming-Qing style shopfronts.",
"s":"Shijiazhuang (30 min from temple): Intercontinental Shijiazhuang (500 CNY/night, international standard, near city center); Hilton Shijiazhuang (450 CNY/night, connected to a shopping mall); Home Inn Shijiazhuang Railway Station (160 CNY/night, basic but clean). Zhengding town has limited accommodation: Zhengding Ancient City Inn (正定古城客栈, 200 CNY/night, inside the old city walls, courtyard style, Chinese only). The best approach: stay in Shijiazhuang, visit Zhengding as a day trip. The HSR connection makes this easy. If coming from Beijing as a long day trip: take the 7am HSR, arrive Shijiazhuang 8am, taxi to Zhengding 8:30am, visit temple 8:30-11:30am, explore old town until 2pm, return to Beijing by 4pm.",
"f":"Zhengding local specialties: Ma Jia Shao Ji (马家烧鸡, Ma family stewed chicken, the town's signature dish since the Qing dynasty, 60 CNY for a whole chicken, sold at shops near the temple main gate); Zhengding beng gan (正定崩肝, crispy fried beef liver strips, local snack, 15-20 CNY); lv rou huo shao (驴肉火烧, donkey meat sandwich -- Hebei's most famous street food, 10-15 CNY, the Baoding style is the most authentic). In Shijiazhuang: Yanfenglou (燕凤楼) has been serving Hebei banquet cuisine since the 1940s (80-120 CNY/person). Near the temple: the approach street (Zhengding South Street) has local noodle shops -- any one serving jiaozi (dumplings, 15-20 CNY for 15 pieces) will be good.",
"se":"Spring (April-May): 15-25C, comfortable. Autumn (September-October): 12-24C, clear skies. Summer (June-August): 28-38C, the temple courtyard has limited shade -- go early morning. Winter: 0-8C, cold but the temple is fully open. Zhengding is genuinely off the foreign tourist circuit -- you will almost certainly be the only non-Chinese visitor here in any season. This is part of its charm. Hours: 8am-5pm (summer), 8:30am-4:30pm (winter). Admission: 50 CNY.",
"ti":"Longxing Temple is one of China's most underrated heritage sites -- four original Song buildings survive here, commissioned personally by Emperor Taizu in 971 AD. The highlight is the Great Compassion Pavilion (Dabeige, 大悲阁) housing a 21.3-meter, 42-arm bronze Guanyin statue cast in 971 -- the tallest surviving Song bronze sculpture anywhere. The sheer scale is overwhelming when you enter the hall. Other Song treasures: the Moni Hall (摩尼殿) has 422 years of painted murals on its walls; the Revolving Library (转轮藏) is an original Song rotating bookshelf mechanism carved from wood -- turn it to gain merit, a uniquely Song Buddhist concept. After the temple: explore Zhengding old city wall (portions of Ming wall survive, free), the Linji Temple (临济寺, 5 min walk, origin site of the Linji/Rinzai school of Zen Buddhism, still active), and the Kaiyuan Temple bell tower (开元寺, Tang-era brick base). Shijiazhuang is not a tourist city -- but Zhengding makes it worth the detour."
},
"qingmingheyuan":{
"t":"Qingming Riverside Landscape Garden (清明上河园) is on the west side of Kaifeng's old city. Fly into Zhengzhou Xinzheng Airport (CGO). From airport: direct bus to Kaifeng (1.5h, 40 CNY) or HSR from Zhengzhou East to Kaifeng North (20 min, 25 CNY). In Kaifeng: Bus 1, 15, 20 to Qingming Riverside Garden (清明上河园). Taxi from anywhere in Kaifeng: 15 min (15 CNY). From Kaifeng Railway Station: Bus 1 (20 min). The park entrance is a large Song-style gate with a plaza -- you cannot miss it.",
"s":"Kaifeng Guesthouse Hotel (350 CNY, inside the park area, Song-themed rooms, most immersive option); Pullman Kaifeng (450 CNY, near Dragon Pavilion scenic area, 10 min drive); Kaifeng Home Inn near railway station (120 CNY, budget clean option). Kaifeng has modest hotel options -- adjust expectations downward from Xi'an or Hangzhou. The Kaifeng Guesthouse Hotel offers rooms decorated in Song dynasty style and is the most atmospheric choice, though amenities are basic (no elevator, Chinese breakfast only). Staying in Kaifeng overnight is recommended -- the night market experience is worth it.",
"f":"The park has Song-themed restaurants: Dongjing Restaurant (东京酒楼) serves dishes recreated from Song cookbooks (80-120 CNY/person, dishes like Dongpo pork, steamed fish in lotus leaf, lotus seed soup). Outside the park: Kaifeng's Drum Tower Night Market (鼓楼夜市, 15 min taxi) is the real culinary destination. Must-try: guan tang bao (灌汤包, soup buns) at Kaifeng Diyilou (开封第一楼, since 1922, 40-60 CNY/basket); tong zi ji (桶子鸡, braised young chicken, 60-80 CNY whole); chao liang fen (炒凉粉, wok-fried mung bean jelly with garlic and chili, 10-15 CNY, a Kaifeng street food specialty). Also: the night market stall selling hua sheng gao (peanut cake -- watch them pound peanuts with giant wooden mallets, 10 CNY per box). For a proper restaurant meal: Huangjia Baozi (黄家包子) competes with Diyilou for Kaifeng's best baozi (40-60 CNY/person).",
"se":"Spring (April-May) and autumn (September-October): 15-25C, best seasons. The daily performance schedule peaks in spring and autumn with 20+ shows. Arrive at 9am opening to catch everything. Key performances: opening ceremony at the main gate (9am), the Water Battle (水战, 11am, Song naval battle recreation on the park lake -- the front-row seats get splashed), Kaifeng Wedding Procession (2pm), Imperial Examination reenactment (3pm), and the evening light-and-water show (7:30pm, separate ticket or combo ticket). Summer (June-August): 28-38C, very hot, but the park has shaded pavilions. Winter: 0-8C, fewer performances, but the Lantern Festival (Feb, dates vary by lunar calendar) transforms the park with elaborate traditional lanterns. Hours: 9am-9pm (summer), 9am-6pm (winter). Admission: 120 CNY (day), 60 CNY (evening after 5pm).",
"ti":"LET US BE CLEAR: This is a 1998 theme park built on empty land with ZERO historical fabric. It is a FULL-SCALE RECONSTRUCTION of the city depicted in Zhang Zeduan's 12th-century scroll painting 'Along the River During the Qingming Festival' (清明上河图), one of China's most famous artworks. The park works best for: families with children (who enjoy the performances), photographers (the buildings and costumes make for striking photos), and anyone wanting to visualize what Song dynasty daily life looked like at human scale. It is essentially a Song dynasty Renaissance fair -- historically informed but entirely modern construction. Budget 4-5 hours minimum to see all the performances and walk the full park (it is large -- 600+ mu / 40 hectares). Dress for walking. If you want GENUINE Song architecture, go to Baoguo Temple (Ningbo, 1013) or Longxing Temple (Zhengding, 971-1032). Qingming Riverside Garden is for the EXPERIENCE, not the authenticity."
},
}
